and objects on top of our real world.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.696
Both of these could completely transform
all areas of life,

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.365
including how we use our environments.

00:01:14.365 --> 00:01:16.910
Now we can all be in the same space,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.413
but have a completely subjective
experience of that space.

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:25.543
But why does all of this
still feel so far from reality?

00:01:32.801 --> 00:01:33.885
Public transit

00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:38.264
is this unique in-between space
where we can physically exist

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:42.727
in a communal space, but mentally be
immersed in our personal worlds.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:45.438
It's also a universal experience,

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:49.609
So it's kind of the best way
to observe cultural changes

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:53.154
But there's something about this image

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:56.699
that's a little bit unsettling,

00:01:57.575 --> 00:02:00.703
and it might have something
to do with ambiguity.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.039
According to Claude Lévi-Strauss,

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:06.793
The facial disguise temporarily
eliminated from social intercourse.

00:02:07.001 --> 00:02:10.880
The part of the body which reveals
personal feelings and attitudes.

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:16.261
The reason why masks can be creepy,
even if it's neutral or happy,

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:20.181
might have something to do
with the fact that it hides the emotions

00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:22.142
or intentions of the person underneath.

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:25.145
I don't know if this person is crying
or glaring at me,

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:29.107
And this weird psychological phenomenon
might be why Apple

00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:32.944
spent millions of dollars
developing this feature called Eyesight,

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:37.240
which at first
seems like a completely useless feature.

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:41.119
It basically allows people
to see an actual life simulation

00:02:41.119 --> 00:02:44.956
of your eyes with data
from the internal infrared cameras

00:02:45.039 --> 00:02:48.877
two per eye, to capture your eye movement
and facial expressions.

00:02:49.127 --> 00:02:53.006
The data is then fed to a machine
learning algorithm that combines it

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:57.635
with your own unique digital persona
using the front facing truedepth camera.

00:02:57.886 --> 00:03:00.889
all of this tech in R&amp;D,
just so that you can see

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:04.893
if somebody is looking directly at you
or if they're immersed in their VR space.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:08.897
And this connection between
what's happening behind your glasses

00:03:08.897 --> 00:03:14.277
and your surroundings could be
one of the key steps to massive option.

00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:18.198
and as we get more comfortable
wearing these devices, it's

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:22.785
going to unlock a whole new world
that straddles between the physical

00:03:22.785 --> 00:03:23.578
and the digital.

00:03:26.664 --> 00:03:28.208
Historically architecture

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:32.170
used programing diagrams
to define distinct separate spaces.

00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:36.049
As we get into more postmodern
architecture, we start to get a bleeding

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:39.636
of different programs
creating these in-between spaces.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:43.973
VR and the air allow us to merge
all of this

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:47.769
and create multiple spaces
within one physical space.

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:52.315
And not just that each person
could have a totally different experience

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:53.483
of that space.

00:03:53.483 --> 00:03:57.946
Basically, everything we know about three
dimensional space goes out the door

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:03.243
and we get what Matsuda calls
the subjective space, where

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:07.872
everyone can be in the same environment
and experience different realities.

00:04:08.206 --> 00:04:11.209
Someone sitting
just next to you on the bus will see

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:15.171
a completely different city curated
to their personal taste and interests.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:19.050
You can be in your digital bedroom
while you're physically in a cubicle

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.718
or an airplane or a prison cell.

00:04:20.718 --> 00:04:23.930
you can also be in a museum
or a concert hall or an aquarium

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.558
or a basketball court
or a pool or a school or a library

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.981
There's something fundamentally human

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:36.985
about wanting to share a communal,
physical experience with other people.

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:40.405
studies have found that going
through a shared experience

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:44.909
just by being in the same space
can intensify that experience,

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:49.289
even if there's no verbal exchange
and even if it's just with strangers.

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:53.334
In this one study from Yale University,
students were given chocolates

00:04:53.543 --> 00:04:57.088
either simultaneously
with another participant or alone.

00:04:57.797 --> 00:05:00.383
Despite both chocolates being identical.

00:05:00.383 --> 00:05:02.802
Students
reported the chocolates tasting better

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:05.346
when the ADA
with the other person in the room.

00:05:05.346 --> 00:05:07.598
And that's why even if we don't need

00:05:07.598 --> 00:05:10.601
to, we like going to a library
or a coffee shop.

00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:14.772
We like being in the same space
in the presence of other people.

00:05:14.939 --> 00:05:19.402
